          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A 22-year-old woman on combined oral contraceptives has a reddish, velvety, gran</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 22-year-old woman on combined oral contraceptives has a reddish, velvety, granular area around the external os on speculum examination. The cervix is smooth, non-indurated, and non-tender. What is the most likely diagnosis?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Cervical ectropion</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Ectropion is oestrogen-driven eversion of columnar epithelium onto the ectocervix, common with COCPs, pregnancy, and adolescence. The smooth, non-indurated cervix confirms it is benign.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Cervicitis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Cervicitis, usually gonococcal or chlamydial, can look similar but is typically accompanied by discharge and inflammation, and needs microbiological exclusion before assuming ectropion.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Cervical polyp</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>A polyp is a discrete pedunculated growth protruding through the os, not a diffuse velvety area covering the ectocervix.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Invasive cervical carcinoma</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Malignancy classically presents with an indurated, fixed cervix — the opposite of the smooth, mobile cervix described here.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The smooth, non-indurated cervix with oestrogen exposure (COCP use) is classic for physiological ectropion, not a polyp, cervicitis, or malignancy."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct — induration is the key differentiator: a smooth cervix favours ectropion, a hard fixed one points to malignancy."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
                "enableSolutionsButton": true,
                "enableCheckButton": true,
