          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which clinical finding is described as pathognomonic for a complete hydatidiform</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which clinical finding is described as pathognomonic for a complete hydatidiform mole?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Passage of grape-like vesicles per vaginum</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Passage of grape-like vesicles is pathognomonic for a complete mole, reflecting hydropic degeneration of chorionic villi.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Hyperemesis gravidarum</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Hyperemesis is common in molar pregnancy due to very high β-hCG cross-reacting with TSH receptors, but it also occurs in normal pregnancy and is not pathognomonic.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Pre-eclampsia before 20 weeks</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Pre-eclampsia before 20 weeks is a classic red flag for a molar pregnancy but can rarely occur in other conditions too, so it is suggestive rather than pathognomonic.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Bilateral theca lutein cysts on ultrasound</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Theca lutein cysts result from excessive ovarian stimulation by β-hCG and are strongly suggestive, but they can occur with other high-hCG states and are not pathognomonic.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Hyperemesis, early pre-eclampsia, and theca lutein cysts are all suggestive of a molar pregnancy, but only the passage of grape-like vesicles is truly pathognomonic."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct — grape-like vesicle passage is the one finding specific enough to be called pathognomonic for a complete mole."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
